Installing Aertun
*****************

From source, or from a release: an attested binary or a container image.


From source
===========

Requirements
------------

Go 1.27 or later.  Nothing else.

This is a Go program with no dependencies: `go.mod` has no require block, so
the standard library and the Go toolchain are the whole build.  There is no
`./configure`, because there is nothing to configure at build time.


Building and installing
-----------------------

     make
     sudo make install

`make install` honours the usual variables:

     sudo make install prefix=/usr/local     # the default
     make install prefix="$HOME/.local"      # no root needed
     sudo make install DESTDIR=/tmp/stage    # for a package build

To remove it:

     sudo make uninstall


Without make
------------

     go build -o aertun ./cmd/aertun
     install -m 0755 aertun /usr/local/bin/


A container from source
-----------------------

     docker build -t aertun .
     docker run --rm -v aertun:/srv aertun --root /srv/store init --owner you@example.com
     docker run -p 8080:8080 -v aertun:/srv aertun

The first command prints the link that adds your passkey; open it once the
second is running.

The base is `gcr.io/distroless/static-debian12:nonroot`: the binary, CA
certificates, a passwd entry and timezone data.  No shell, no package manager,
no interpreter, no libc.  It runs as nonroot, and carries LICENSE and NOTICE in
/usr/share/doc/aertun.


From a release
==============

Each release publishes one static binary for each of linux, macOS and Windows
on amd64 and arm64, with SHA256SUMS, LICENSE and NOTICE beside them, and a
container image, ghcr.io/aertun/aertun, tagged with the version without its
`v`: the git tag v1.2.3 publishes 1.2.3, 1.2 and latest.

Check a binary before you run it:

     sha256sum -c --ignore-missing SHA256SUMS
     gh attestation verify aertun-linux-amd64 --repo aertun/aertun

The checksum proves the file matches the list.  The attestation proves the
file was built by this repository's release workflow, from a tag, and names
which one.


Checking the build
==================

     make check

That runs the whole test suite: one hundred and eleven packages, including several that
walk this program's own source and fail when a command has no screen or no
machine interface without a written reason, or declares no privilege.

A test counts the packages and fails naming the right number when this one is
wrong.


A first run
===========

     aertun init --owner you@example.com
     aertun serve

Then open the link `init` printed, add a passkey, and follow Get started.


Watching it run
===============

`aertun serve` answers /healthz (it is serving) and /readyz (it can do its
work: the policy reads and the audit log takes records), with no
credential and no detail.  /metrics is the Prometheus text format and
answers a bearer token only, read from the request and never from the
server's own environment:

     aertun token issue prometheus --principal prometheus --role viewer

Give that token to the scrape as its bearer credential.  Agents' tokens
are refused there.  The metrics count by closed words only (status
classes, outcomes, job names) and name no person, agent or tool.


Backups
=======

     aertun backup --out /backups/aertun-$(date +%F).aertun-backup --passphrase-file /etc/aertun/backup-pass
     aertun restore /backups/aertun-2026-11-20.aertun-backup --to /srv/aertun-restored --passphrase-file F

A backup is the whole store, keys and secrets included, in one file
encrypted with AES-256-GCM under a key derived from the passphrase (PBKDF2
with SHA-256, 600,000 iterations).  The passphrase is at least fifteen
characters, read from a 0600 file or AERTUN_BACKUP_PASSPHRASE; keep it
apart from the backups, because without it they cannot be read.  Taking
one is an administrator's act and is recorded in the audit log.

The server need not stop.  The audit log and the receipt log are measured
under their locks and copied to exactly that length, so each is a whole
prefix; everything else is written whole by design.

A restore goes into a new, empty directory and never over a store.  It
then rebuilds the audit log's chain and Merkle tree and holds them to the
head recorded at backup, to every head the log signed, and to every root
it anchored in Bitcoin, and it checks the receipt log.  A restore that
fails those checks says so and the directory must not be put in service;
one that fails part way leaves the directory empty.  An audit log kept in
another directory is restored beside the store.

Recovery point: whatever was written since the last backup, so schedule
backups as often as you can afford to lose (hourly, from cron or a timer).
Recovery time: one read of the file and one rebuild of the audit tree;
measure your own with `time aertun restore` on a copy, and repeat it, since
a backup nobody has restored is a hope.  The test suite restores one on
every change.

